  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Saying no politely is a big step towards real conversation. Luganda makes a verb negative with a small prefix.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Saagala kaawa.", "base": "I don’t want coffee." },
      { "target": "Sirya nnyama.", "base": "I don’t eat meat." },
      { "target": "Simanyi, ssebo.", "base": "I don’t know, sir." },
      { "target": "Sitegeera. Ddamu.", "base": "I don’t understand. Repeat." },
      { "target": "Sirina ssente.", "base": "I don’t have money." },
      { "target": "Tewali mazzi.", "base": "There is no water." },
      { "target": "Totya!", "base": "Don’t be afraid!" }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "negatives",
        "title": "si-, to-, ta-",
        "body": [
          "Put **si-** in front for “I don’t”: **simanyi**, **sirina**, **sigenda**. When the verb starts with a vowel, the sounds merge: **si + yagala** becomes **saagala**.",
          "For “you don’t” use **to-** (**toyagala**), for “he/she doesn’t” use **ta-** (**tagenda**), and for “we don’t” use **tetu-** (**tetulina**). A **to-** command means “don’t!”: **Togenda!**, **Totya!**",
          "After a negative verb, the noun usually drops its first vowel: **Sinywa mata** (not *amata*), **Tewali mazzi**."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["", "Yes", "No"],
          ["I", "njagala", "saagala"],
          ["you", "oyagala", "toyagala"],
          ["he / she", "ayagala", "tayagala"],
          ["we", "twagala", "tetwagala"],
          ["there is", "waliwo", "tewali"]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "A soft no",
        "body": [
          "A flat **Nedda** can feel abrupt. People soften a refusal with thanks and a reason: **Weebale, naye saagala kaawa** (thank you, but I don’t want coffee). Refusing food entirely can seem unfriendly, so taking a little is kind."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
